I think the short answer is: don't even try it.

The main driver for MC interconnect is rm.mod(there's actually three
separate
kernel modules that comprise the MC driver).  This file lives with the
cluster
.mods (/usr/opt/TruCluster/sys/), which, I believe, means one would need a
cluster license...  If one has a cluster license then run the database on
one node
and app on the other in a cluster configuration...  One can use ICSNET to
perform standard TCP/IP like communication between nodes over ics0.

For laughs lets say, one was able to get the .mods, and link the kernel,
and get it to boot, how would the two nodes communicate?  The memory channel
driver does not speak TCP/IP... Furthermore, the MC driver is designed to be
a
cluster interconnect and as such behaves, um... not like a normal device..
the nodes
can do things like panic each other, and without rest of the cluster
infrastructure bye
bye database node hello pissed off customer...

I do not recommend this configuration.

Tell the customer to get a TruCluster license.

James.


