On Wed, 29 Jan 2003, Joe Chung wrote:

> andy thomas <andy@ic.ac.uk> writes:
> > We have a server running DU 4.0D and samba 2.2.7 which among other things
> > is a fileserver for a number of Windows NT/2000/XP PC clients. From time
> > to time, a user complains about strange files apearing in his/her home
> > directory and the ls command list these as the filename '?'. Using the -b
> > option to ls gives the octal equivalent of these files with non-printable
> > names, etc \201, \214, etc. But how can I use these octal file pointers as
> > an argument to the mv command? I'd like to be able to type something like:
> >
> > 	mv '\201' recovered_file.1
> >
> > Does anyone have any ideas?
> >
> > thanks in advance for any help,
> >
> > Andy
> >
>
> Dired mode from within [X]Emacs works every time on file names
> with weird characters.
>
> Start up [X]Emacs,
> Type ESC-X dired [ENTER],
> Enter the name of the directory,
> Scroll down to the file you want to rename,
> Type R "Note - uppercase 'R'",
> Enter the new name,
> Ctrl-x Ctrl-c to exit emacs.

Brilliant!! Thanks a lot, that did the trick. I also found it was possible
to delete strange files too.

