Fred Zwarts wrote:

>Last week I found the /var partition of my Tru64 5.1 system completely filled.
>It turned out that this was due to quite some huge files in /var/tcb/files/dblogs.
>They had names of the form log.DDDDD, where D is a decimal digit.
>I removed these files, after which the /var partition had 90% free space again.
>I wonder, however, whether this was a good action. Where are these files used for
>and what is the best way to cleanup the occupied disk space?
>
>        F.Z.
>
Yes - there are "better" ways to deal with those files.  They are essentially there for history to allow a rebuild of your Enhanced Security "auth.db" file just in case it becomes corrupt.  You can "trim" and "archive" them using the provided tools. Check out the man pages for db_archive & db_checkpoint.  That will point you to secconfig where one of the questions during the processing to create the security configuration was whether or not you wanted to automatically trim your dblogs files.  My guess is you missed the question or said no when you first went from Base to Enhanced Security. 

I would strongly suggest you get all the latest patches - especially for Enhanced Security. The number of times the code writes to the log file has been reduced in later patches and releases.  That obviously cuts down on the number and size of the files. Whether or not the patches specifically state that's what they do, I cannot remember - it's been quite a while since they were created.

-- 

