In article <R6SX9.377$84.113361@localhost>, "Shawn Kearley" <skearley@newfoundlandpower.com> wrote:
>
>My Network person here has indicates that he is planning to replace the
>entire WAN network with new CISCO routers

If your network person employs Cisco's HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol), 
then you can configure your server with a single static default route to the 
"virtual" address of the "router cluster"; no dynamic routing required on the 
server.

>With that change, he is planning to get rid of the RIP protocol
>that we are now using for a better routing protocol.

No longer using RIP for the main dynamic routing protocol is a fine idea.  
However, if needed, the Cisco routers can be configured to "redistribute" the 
information from one routing protocol to another.  So if they decide not to 
use HSRP, your folks should be able to use EIGRP for all the routing 
equipment, but then advertise the routes (but not accept updates with) with 
RIP to the segments your Unix servers are on.

These are the options that occur to me off-hand; there are likely others :)


