Snip3r wrote:
> Hi,
>
> i made this post some time ago, excuse me but i can't find a solution.
>
> On a trucluster 5.1 environment with 2 node i'm trying
> to put postgresql db server in high availibility using the
> example script for ORACLE, but it don't migrate or restart
> and require always 'human intervention' .
>
>
> The error in the syslog is exactly :
>
> "postgresql on member node1 has experienced an unrecoverable failure.
> Human Intervention required to resume its availability."
>
> The script works when launched by hand and works when launched the
> first time via sysman, in the caa section. But it don't work when
> happens a failure.
>
> After the failure this is the result of the command 'caa_stat' :
>
> NAME=pgsql
> TYPE=application
> TARGET=ONLINE
> STATE=UNKNOWN on node1
>
> It's in an UNKNOW state.
>
> All other resource in caa are working well.
>
> Any idea is welcomed.
>
> Thanks.
>
> S.

Well... I'm certainly no *expert* on caa, but we do run a number of large
24x7 Oracle databases under caa with no issues.  AFAIK, all that UNKNOWN
means it what you said - there has been a failure and the database is
neither ONLINE nor OFFLINE. What you need to do is ensure that your 'check'
section of the caa 'pgsql_scr' script for this is written so that it can
recognize the fact that there has been a failure, and then caa will attempt
to 'start' it on the other node.

Do you have caa_stop and caa_start working for the database?  I believe that
you should be able to do a caa_stop and get the instance down to an OFFLINE
target and state.  After that, does caa_start bring it back online?  Seems
to me if start and stop both work, but failures in the database aren't
recognized, you need to flesh out your 'check' option so that it knows how
to tell that it the database is down. Be careful with it, tho, cuz if you
get it wrong and the check fails when in fact nothing is wrong, the database
will 'ping-pong' back and forth between nodes... and it's not a pretty
sight. <g>

FWIW, the Oracle example script was declared 'very simple' by our DBA and
had to be reworked quite a bit before our company had a brain-fart and
decided to release all consultants, of which he was one.  [sigh]  What
changes did you make to it for Postgresql?

-craig




