"Paul Szabo" <psz@maths.usyd.edu.au> wrote in message
news:3E2355D6.794B@maths.usyd.edu.au...
> Daniel Berger wrote:
> > Is there a way to trim wtmp? Mine goes back years and has gotten huge.
>
> What we do, yearly, is (at a quiet time):
>
>   cp -ip /usr/adm/wtmp /usr/adm/wtmp.2002
>   echo -n '' > /usr/adm/wtmp
>
> so the system has a nice, small (empty) file to fill up again.

Log rotation is an art form. Take a look at this approach:

    mv /usr/adm/wtmp /usr/adm/wtmp.{backup name}
    kill -HUP {syslog process ID}

This way, you restart the syslog daemon correctly and the data continues to
pour into the file descriptor of the original file even in its new location
until you restart it.




