"Shawn Kearley" <skearley@newfoundlandpower.com> wrote in message
news:b5ES9.12981$i%.2537218@localhost...
> Hi all:
>
> I am relatively new to Tru64 and UNIX in general, but have recently been
> given the task of managing our Tru64 UNIX systems.  I am now in the
process
> of planning an upgrade of these systems from 4.0f to 5.1A.
>
> What I am wondering is, is it possible to take a full system backup of all
> file systems while the system is in multi-user mode.  These systems are
> required to be operational 24x365, and I cannot take them down to perform
a
> stand-alone backup on any of the systems.  From what I have been told,
> however, is that if you attempt to restore the '/' and '/usr' partitions
> from a backup while the system was in multi-user mode, the restore file
> systems will be corrupted.

Legato is your friend. It has a fairly nice "cache" mode that can be used to
*very quickly* create dumps vulnerable files to local hard-drive dump
images, then transfer those to tape.

Most admins for most OS's I've dealt with would avoid restoring / and /usr
from dump tapes unless they had to: most of us try to keep lists of key
changes from the default OS installation, and find it easier to implement
those changes on a cleanly installed new OS rather than rely on system dumps
of systems that becomre more obsolete and more vulnerable to corruption over
time. And system downtime is a great opportunity to do that OS update you've
been waiting to do, to add those new disk arrays, etc.




